7. ANALYSIS

The alert was generated because the host Murray executed a Docker build command using the image name malicious-docker:latest. This activity occurred shortly after a reported brute-force attempt from 37.19.200.2, creating a possible link between unauthorized access and container-based execution.

The playbook findings suggest that an attacker may have accessed the host, obtained an attack tool from GitHub, and used Docker to run a malicious image. Deploying a container can be abused to execute unauthorized code, although Docker image creation is also a normal administrative and development activity.

The analyst note presents conflicting findings. It states that the relevant IP addresses were checked and were not considered malicious, that the GitHub link merely triggered the alert, and that firewall, proxy, and network logs showed no suspicious downloads or unusual activity. However, a lack of suspicious network traffic does not independently prove that the Docker image was benign, just as a suspicious image name does not prove that it was malicious.

The key unresolved questions are whether the brute-force attempt resulted in a successful login, whether the Docker image was actually built and executed, and whether its contents included malicious code. The image's Dockerfile, layers, digest, runtime history, and associated endpoint events should be examined alongside authentication and process logs.

Assessment: The available evidence is insufficient to confirm malicious container deployment or confidently validate the analyst's false-positive conclusion. The alert should remain under investigation until the contradictory findings are resolved.

